type Category struct {
	Name     string
	Children []*Category
}

func FindPath(root *Category, target string) []string

a:
DFS with backtracking: push the node's name onto `path`, return true if it matches or if any child finds it, otherwise pop and return false.

```go
type Category struct {
	Name     string
	Children []*Category
}

func FindPath(root *Category, target string) []string {
	var path []string
	var dfs func(c *Category) bool
	dfs = func(c *Category) bool {
		path = append(path, c.Name)
		if c.Name == target {
			return true
		}
		for _, ch := range c.Children {
			if dfs(ch) {
				return true
			}
		}
		path = path[:len(path)-1]
		return false
	}
	if root == nil {
		return nil
	}
	for _, ch := range root.Children {
		if dfs(ch) {
			return path
		}
	}
	return nil
}
```

Verified output for `"OLED"`:
```text
Бытовая техника > Телевизоры > OLED
```

**Complexity**: O(N) time (each node visited at most once). O(H) extra space for the recursion stack and path, where H is the tree height.

Notes:
- The root itself is excluded from the path to match the expected output. Clarify this with the interviewer.
- For very deep trees use an explicit stack with parent pointers instead of recursion.
- If lookups are frequent, precompute a `map[name]*node` plus parent links. Then a path is O(H) per query after an O(N) build.
- BFS would also work and finds the shallowest match, but it needs a parent map to rebuild the path.

