a:
Run one function in a goroutine and ping-pong on **one unbuffered channel**. Each unbuffered send/receive pair is a rendezvous, so the two goroutines strictly alternate:

```go
package main

import "fmt"

var ch = make(chan struct{})

func printFoo() {
	for i := 0; i < 10; i++ {
		fmt.Print("Foo")
		ch <- struct{}{}
		<-ch
	}
}

func printBar() {
	for i := 0; i < 10; i++ {
		<-ch
		fmt.Print("Bar\n")
		ch <- struct{}{}
	}
}

func main() {
	go printFoo()
	printBar()
}
```

Output (verified, also with `-race`): `FooBar` ten times.

How it works: Foo prints, then blocks on `ch <-` until Bar receives. Bar prints `Bar\n` and sends back, while Foo waits in `<-ch` before its next iteration. So `Foo` never runs ahead of `Bar`.

Why not a buffered channel: with a buffer, Foo's send would complete without waiting and Foo could print twice in a row. The single-channel constraint relies on unbuffered handoff semantics. The "classic" version uses two channels (one per direction), which is easier to reason about.

