type Cache interface {
	Get(k string) (v string, ok bool)
	Set(k, v string)
}

a:
Basic version: map + RWMutex (reads dominate, `Get` doesn't mutate).

```go
type MapCache struct {
	mu sync.RWMutex
	m  map[string]string
}

func (c *MapCache) Get(k string) (string, bool) {
	c.mu.RLock()
	defer c.mu.RUnlock()
	v, ok := c.m[k]
	return v, ok
}

func (c *MapCache) Set(k, v string) {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	c.m[k] = v
}
```

Bounded LRU: map to list element, move on access, evict from the back. Now `Get` mutates recency, so it needs a full `Mutex`.

```go
type entry struct{ k, v string }

type LRU struct {
	mu    sync.Mutex
	cap   int
	ll    *list.List
	items map[string]*list.Element
}

func (c *LRU) Get(k string) (string, bool) {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	e, ok := c.items[k]
	if !ok {
		return "", false
	}
	c.ll.MoveToFront(e)
	return e.Value.(*entry).v, true
}

func (c *LRU) Set(k, v string) {
	c.mu.Lock()
	defer c.mu.Unlock()
	if e, ok := c.items[k]; ok {
		e.Value.(*entry).v = v
		c.ll.MoveToFront(e)
		return
	}
	c.items[k] = c.ll.PushFront(&entry{k, v})
	if c.ll.Len() > c.cap {
		old := c.ll.Back()
		c.ll.Remove(old)
		delete(c.items, old.Value.(*entry).k)
	}
}
```

Follow-ups:
- **Mutex vs RWMutex**: RW only when reads dominate and don't mutate; LRU `Get` mutates. Under heavy load, **shard** (N caches by key hash).
- **defer**: unlocks on panic and every return path; since Go 1.14 open-coded defers cost almost nothing.
- **Allocations**: a `list.Element` + `*entry` per insert, boxing into `any`, map growth, the key strings. The unbounded map version is the "leak": it grows forever, and Go maps **never shrink** after deletes (true with Swiss tables too); substring keys can pin large parent strings; expired TTL entries linger without a janitor.
- **Avoid allocations**: preallocate `[]entry` of capacity N and link entries by `int32` indexes (intrusive list in a slice), map `string` to `int32`; reuse slots on eviction.
- **Structure**: FIFO eviction needs only a **ring buffer** (O(1), zero alloc). LRU needs O(1) move-to-front, so a **doubly linked list** (ideally index-based inside a slice). A plain slice gives O(n) removals.

