One goroutine per plate, each writing only its own index, and a WaitGroup to know when all channels are closed.

```go
func MonitorPlates(channels []chan bool) []int {
	counts := make([]int, len(channels))
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i, ch := range channels {
		wg.Go(func() {
			pressed := false
			for v := range ch {
				if v && !pressed {
					counts[i]++
				}
				pressed = v
			}
		})
	}
	wg.Wait()
	return counts
}
```

Points to make:
- `counts[i]` is a distinct memory location per goroutine, so no mutex is needed, and `wg.Wait()` makes all writes visible before returning.
- A press is counted on the **rising edge** (false to true), so a repeated `true` without a release isn't double-counted. If the spec guarantees strict alternation, `if v { counts[i]++ }` is enough. Clarify this with the interviewer.
- `range ch` exits when the channel is closed, which is the "all closed" condition.
- Alternative without per-plate goroutines: a single `select` loop over a dynamic set (`reflect.Select`), or fan-in into one channel of `(plate, value)` events processed by one goroutine.
- A nil channel in the slice would block forever; validate input.

Test: plate k gets k+1 press/release pairs, then `true, true, close`, giving `[2 3 4]`.

